    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n- [X] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version\n\n### Module(s)\n\nstarter\n\n### Description\n\nWhen moving to the above director in nvim-tree, mini.starter sends a notification saying \"`buf_id` in `refresh()` is not an identifier of valid Starter buffer\". This occurs whether starter is open or not.\n\n### Neovim version\n\n0.8.1\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. Run `nvim -nu minimal.lua`.\r\n2. Run `:NvimTreeToggle`.\r\n3. Move up and press enter to move up a directory.\r\n\r\nminimal.lua:\r\n```\r\nlocal root = vim.fn.fnamemodify(\"./.minimal\", \":p\")\r\nfor _, name in ipairs({ \"config\", \"data\", \"state\", \"cache\" }) do\r\n\tvim.env[(\"XDG_%s_HOME\"):format(name:upper())] = root .. \"/\" .. name\r\nend\r\nlocal lazypath = root .. \"/plugins/lazy.nvim\"\r\nif not vim.loop.fs_stat(lazypath) then\r\n\tvim.fn.system({\r\n\t\t\"git\",\r\n\t\t\"clone\",\r\n\t\t\"--filter=blob:none\",\r\n\t\t\"--single-branch\",\r\n\t\t\"https://github.com/folke/lazy.nvim.git\",\r\n\t\tlazypath,\r\n\t})\r\nend\r\nvim.opt.runtimepath:prepend(lazypath)\r\nrequire(\"lazy\").setup({\r\n\t{\r\n\t\t\"echasnovski/mini.starter\",\r\n\t\tconfig = function()\r\n\t\t\tlocal starter = require(\"mini.starter\")\r\n\t\t\tstarter.setup({\r\n\t\t\t\titems = { starter.sections.recent_files(10, true) },\r\n\t\t\t})\r\n\t\tend,\r\n\t},\r\n\t{\r\n\t\t\"nvim-tree/nvim-tree.lua\",\r\n\t\tconfig = function()\r\n\t\t\trequire(\"nvim-tree\").setup()\r\n\t\tend,\r\n\t},\r\n}, {\r\n\troot = root .. \"/plugins\",\r\n})\r\n```\n\n### Expected behavior\n\n_No response_\n\n### Actual behavior\n\nA notification is sent by mini.starter saying \"`buf_id` in `refresh()` is not an identifier of valid Starter buffer\". This does not occur with the default mini.starter setup, the `items` line in the given `minimal.lua` is required to reproduce the issue.",
